Prevent arbitrary iomem mapping

When Xen is locked down, check whether the iomem being mapped belongs to a
device assigned to the target domain. This prevents a malicious dom0 userspace
mapping iomem into a domain and then using it to control a device to attack
dom0 kernel space.

In current vGPU implementation card is owned by dom0 but guests has also
access to card memory.
Currently if secure boot is enabled memory can be accessed only by
assigned domain.
Allow Nidia GPU cards memory to be shared with other guests to make vGPU
work.

The list of card device IDs was extracted from vgpuConfig.xml
configuration file using this file (stripped down to put into this commit
message, original script had many more checks):

#!/usr/bin/env python3
import xml.dom.minidom

fn = 'vgpuConfig.xml'
doc = xml.dom.minidom.parseString(open(fn).read())

ids = set()
for p in doc.getElementsByTagName('pgpu'):
    dev = p.getElementsByTagName('devId')[0]
    v = dev.getAttribute('vendorId')
    dev = dev.getAttribute('deviceId')
    assert v.lower() == '0x10de' and dev != ''
    if p.getAttribute('hostVgpuMode') != 'sriov':
        ids.add(dev.lower())
print('\n'.join(sorted(ids)))

